Chains: SDD

Purpose:
  Define how engineering work moves through dependent engineering stages,
  from task creation to final verification. This is the WHEN/ORDER layer
  (see skills/skills.sdd -> Purpose for the full four-layer map:
  project/architecture/chains/skills).

  Chains exist because a single requested stage is rarely the whole
  unit of engineering work. AI MUST NOT treat completing one stage as
  completing the feature — the chain continues until every required
  downstream stage, including VR, has passed (see Rules [C19] below).
  This is the core reason this file exists, not an incidental rule.


Owns: ./

Scope:
  Chain model
  Stage vocabulary
  Stage dependency rules
  Chain transition rules
  Chain validation
  Failure recovery
  Parallel execution rules


DoesNotOwn:
  Project-specific flows
  Project-specific dependencies
  Project-specific tasks
  Engineering skill implementations
  Project architecture

  (see architecture/architecture.sdd -> SeparationOfConcerns — HOW work
  is implemented belongs to skills/; WHICH flow a real project actually
  runs belongs to project/flows.sdd and project/<domain>/flows.sdd, not
  here. This file only defines the reusable vocabulary and rules a real
  project flow is built from — see ProjectFlow and FlowResolution below.)


Principle:
  A chain represents the execution order of engineering work.
  A chain MUST be dependency-driven, not hardcoded per project.
  A chain MUST NOT assume every project uses the same engineering stages
  — see MobileExample below.

  Root .sdd keeps reusable rules. A project keeps its real model, flow
  and dependencies (see ProjectFlow below). This file MUST NOT grow a
  subdirectory per chain type or per stage — a single Stages/DefaultFlow
  section already says that once (see NoDuplication; see Note for why
  STEP 11's ./stages/ + ./templates/ split was reverted here).


Stages:

  Chain-orchestration stages — analysis/decision points that begin or
  redirect a chain. Their exact meaning can differ by what triggered the
  chain (new work vs. a bug vs. a change), so they are named here but
  scoped by the real chain in ProjectFlow, not fixed in advance:

    AN:     Analysis         — initial scope/requirement analysis.
    AR:     Architecture     — architecture decisions for new work.
    IMPACT: Impact Analysis  — determine what a bug or change actually
                               affects before touching it.

  Engineering discipline stages — one responsibility each, reusable
  across every project:

    DB:  Database          — schema/data changes.
    BE:  Backend           — backend implementation.
    API: API / Contract    — API contract and integration.
    FE:  Frontend          — web frontend implementation.
    MD:  Mobile            — mobile implementation.
    QA:  Quality Assurance — testing and quality validation.
    DO:  DevOps            — build, deployment, infra validation.
    VR:  Verification      — final system verification.

  A stage is a name and a responsibility, not a file — see Note for why
  a dedicated file per stage or per chain type was dropped.


Important:
  VR = VERIFY.
  VR is the final validation stage of a chain.
  VR MUST NOT be treated as an implementation stage.


ChainLifecycle:
  DISCOVER > PLAN > EXECUTE > REVIEW > VERIFY

  Note:
    This is the macro lifecycle a chain moves through as a whole — not
    the same thing as a chain's concrete Sequence (see ProjectFlow below)
    and not the same thing as a single task's own lifecycle (see
    tasks/tasks.sdd -> Lifecycle, which is per-task, not per-chain).


ExecutionModel:
  Chain > Resolve stages > Resolve dependencies > Resolve required skills
  > Execute eligible stage > Validate stage > Continue chain


Dependency:
  requires:    stage cannot execute until the dependency is satisfied
  blocks:      an unresolved stage prevents a dependent stage from starting
  optional:    a stage may be skipped once proven unnecessary (never
               silently — see Rules [C7]/[C9])
  parallel:    stages may execute concurrently when independent
  invalidates: a change requires downstream reevaluation (see Rules [C15])


DefaultFlow:
  AN > AR > DB > BE > API > FE > MD > QA > DO > VR

  Note:
    A reference order only, illustrating how the Stages above typically
    depend on each other — not a real project's chain (see Principle,
    ProjectFlow below). A real chain skips/reorders/adds stages per its
    own dependencies (e.g. a bugfix chain has no AR; MD only appears
    when mobile is affected — see MobileExample).


ProjectFlow:
  A project's actual chain — which stages really apply, in what real
  order, for which kind of work (feature / bugfix / change / ...) — is
  owned by the project model, not by this file (see DoesNotOwn):

    Project-wide default: @../project/flows.sdd
    Domain-specific:      @../project/<domain>/flows.sdd

  Neither file exists yet in this repo — .sdd/ is the only hand-built
  tree here (see PROJECT.sdd -> Note), and a real flow is real project
  content with no concrete domain to belong to yet. This is a forward
  reference, not a missing file.


FlowResolution:
  Global chain rules (this file)
    > Project flow (../project/flows.sdd)
    > Domain flow (../project/<domain>/flows.sdd)
    > Feature-specific dependencies
    > Final executable chain for that prompt


DynamicFlow:
  Prompt > ImpactAnalysis > DetermineAffectedStages > ResolveDependencies
  > BuildChain > Execute

  Note:
    This is how a chain is actually assembled for a real prompt — see
    FlowResolution above for how this file's generic rules and a
    project's own flow are reconciled when they need it.


Illustrations:

  MobileExample:
    If mobile is not affected: BE > API > FE > QA > DO > VR
    If mobile is affected:     BE > API > FE > MD > QA > DO > VR

  ParallelExample:
    If DB and BE are independent of each other:
         DB
          |
          +------+
          |      |
          v      v
         BE     API
          |      |
          +--+---+
             |
             v
             FE


Failure:
  Stage > FAILED > ImpactAnalysis > ResponsibleStage > RECOVER > ContinueChain

ReviewFailure:
  Stage > REVIEW > FAILED > ResponsibleStage > RECOVER

VerificationFailure:
  VR > FAILED > ImpactAnalysis > AffectedStage(s) > RECOVER > QA > DO > VR


Completion:
  A chain is DONE only when:
    all required stages are completed
    all required reviews have passed
    all required dependencies are satisfied
    VR has passed


Rules:

  [C1]  Every executable task MUST belong to a chain.
  [C2]  Every chain MUST define its stages as an ordered Sequence (see
        ProjectFlow above — a real project's flow, not this file, is
        where that Sequence is written down).
  [C3]  Every stage MUST have a declared responsibility (see Stages above).
  [C4]  Stage order MUST be derived from dependencies, not hardcoded
        identically for every project (see Dependency above).
  [C5]  A stage MUST NOT execute before its required dependencies are
        satisfied.
  [C6]  A stage MAY execute in parallel with another stage when their
        dependencies allow it (see ParallelExample).
  [C7]  A chain MUST NOT silently skip a required stage.
  [C8]  Optional stages MUST declare their activation condition (see
        Dependency -> optional).
  [C9]  A stage MAY be dynamically added when impact analysis identifies
        a new requirement, and MAY be removed when proven irrelevant —
        never silently (see [C7]).
  [C10] Completing one stage MUST NOT automatically complete the entire
        chain.
  [C11] Chain completion requires VR to pass (see Completion above).
  [C12] A failed stage MUST return to its responsible stage for recovery
        (see Failure above).
  [C13] A failed review MUST reopen the responsible stage, not just the
        review step (see ReviewFailure above).
  [C14] VR failure MUST trigger impact analysis before recovery (see
        VerificationFailure above).
  [C15] A completed stage MUST be reopened when a later change
        invalidates its output (see Dependency -> invalidates).
  [C16] Chain definitions MUST NOT contain engineering implementation
        rules (see architecture/architecture.sdd -> SeparationOfConcerns
        — HOW belongs to skills/, not here).
  [C17] Chain stages MAY reference skills.
  [C18] Chain stages MAY reference project models.
  [C19] AI MUST preserve chain continuity — completing the originally
        requested stage MUST NOT be treated as completing the feature
        when downstream stages remain (see Purpose above; this is the
        rule that gives this file its reason to exist).
  [C20] Chain decisions MUST be traceable.
  [C21] Global (engine-level) chain changes require human approval (see
        PROJECT.sdd -> HumanDecision); project-specific chain/flow
        definitions belong to the project model, not here (see
        DoesNotOwn above, ProjectFlow above).


Navigation:
  Project flow (repo-wide): @../project/flows.sdd
  Domain flow:               @../project/<domain>/flows.sdd
  Tasks:                     @../tasks/
  Skills:                    @../skills/
  State:                     @../state/


State: +

Note:
  (prompt/new/34.md, STEP 12) STEP 11's `./stages/` (one file per
  engineering-discipline stage) and `./templates/` (one file per chain
  type) subdirectories are removed here — deleted in this pass
  (preserved in git history, full STEP 11 rationale included there).
  They were unnecessary abstraction: this file already states the Stage
  vocabulary once, and a chain TYPE (feature/bugfix/change/...) is not
  a reusable engine concept — it is one project's own choice of which
  stages it runs and in what order, which belongs in that project's own
  flow, not in a fixed set of global templates (see Principle above,
  DoesNotOwn above).

  This reinstates the STEP 5 precedent of chains.sdd as a single file,
  but goes further: STEP 5's router still dispatched to per-type
  `.chain` files at engine level; here there is no chain-type dispatch
  in this file at all. `DefaultFlow` is one reference order, and which
  stages a real chain actually runs is resolved entirely through
  `ProjectFlow`/`FlowResolution` above — including what used to be
  chain-type-specific dependencies (IMPACT for bugfix/change), which
  now fold into "Feature-specific dependencies" in `FlowResolution`
  rather than a dedicated global file per chain type.

  Deliberate departures from the source spec (prompt/new/34.md), applied
  per the standing "apply, don't copy" rule:

  Kept this file's existing richer structure (`ChainLifecycle`,
  `ExecutionModel`, `Dependency`, `DynamicFlow`, `Illustrations`,
  `Failure`/`ReviewFailure`/`VerificationFailure`, `Completion`, the
  full `[C1]-[C21]` Rules) rather than replacing it with the source's
  shorter draft — the source's objection was specifically to the extra
  `./stages/`/`./templates/` subdirectories ("chains/stages bunlar
  yoxdur", the source's own words), not to these sections, which stay
  useful and add no extra files.

  Source's `ProjectFlow:`/`FlowResolution:` sections were adopted (new
  in this file) — a genuine improvement over STEP 11: they state the
  precedence between this file's generic rules and a real project's own
  flow explicitly, where STEP 11 only implied it through `Templates:` +
  `DoesNotOwn`.

  Source's flat `Stages:` list (`AN = Analysis`, one line each, no
  responsibility text) was kept split into orchestration vs. discipline
  stages, each with a one-line responsibility — dropping the
  responsibility text would have thrown away real content this file
  already had, for no simplification benefit the source itself needed.

  Source's `Example:`/`Mobile affected:`/`Parallel:` sections were kept
  under the existing `Illustrations:` heading (`MobileExample`/
  `ParallelExample`) rather than duplicated under new names — same
  content, no reason to rename.

  Source's `Spec: ChainSystem` header, bare `Owns:` topic list, and
  `Resolution:` naming were not reinstated — already reconsidered once
  during STEP 11 for repo-wide-convention reasons (see git history) and
  those reasons still apply unchanged.
